course: physics
lesson: 2
title: The Moon, the planets and the pull that holds them
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Swing a ball on a string round your head: the string keeps pulling the ball towards your hand and bends its path into a circle.
explain: The Moon is like that ball, and the Earth's gravity is the string: the Moon is falling towards us all the time, but it moves sideways so fast that it keeps missing.
explain: The same pull that drops an apple holds the Moon round the Earth and the Earth round the Sun; Isaac Newton was the first to show it is one and the same pull.
explain: Astronauts float in the space station not because gravity is gone, but because the station and everyone in it are falling round the Earth together.
explain: Your weight is how hard a planet pulls you, so on the small Moon you would weigh about one sixth as much, while your amount of stuff, your mass, stays the same.
fact: The Moon is falling towards the Earth all the time, but its sideways speed carries it past, so its path bends round the Earth.
fact: If the Earth's gravity switched off, the Moon would fly away in a straight line.
fact: Let go of a ball swung on a string and it flies off in a straight line, not in a curve.
fact: Up at the space station, gravity is still almost as strong as on the ground.
fact: Astronauts float because they and the station are falling round the Earth together, so nothing presses on the floor.
fact: On the Moon you would weigh about one sixth of your weight on Earth, and on Jupiter more than twice as much.
fact: Weight is how hard a planet pulls you; mass is how much stuff you are made of, and it does not change from planet to planet.
fact: Bathroom scales measure how hard you press on them: the spring inside squashes more when you press harder.
fact: In a lift that starts to go up, scales show more for a moment; in one that starts to go down, they show less.
fact: The Sun's gravity holds the Earth, and the Earth's sideways speed keeps it from falling into the Sun.
fact: Satellites that tell your phone where you are fall round the Earth like little moons, high above the air, with no engine needed; if one slows down too much, it drops into the air and burns up.
fact: A shooting star is not a star: it is a bit of space rock, often no bigger than a grain of sand, glowing as it rushes into the air, because the air in front of it gets squashed and very hot.
fact: Water stays in a bucket swung fast over your head because the bucket's bottom keeps pushing it round the circle.
fact: A roller coaster car stays on the track upside down in a loop because it is going fast enough that the track has to keep pushing it round.
exercise: Why doesn't the Moon fall down onto the Earth? | It moves sideways so fast that it keeps missing | There is no gravity where the Moon is | The Moon is held up there by the Sun's bright light | The Moon is much lighter than air
exercise: Is the Moon falling towards the Earth? | Yes, all the time, but it keeps missing | No, it is floating still in space | No, it is being pushed away from us by the Sun | Only during a full moon
exercise: You swing a ball on a string round your head. What keeps it moving in a circle? | The string pulling it towards your hand | The air pushing it round | The ball wanting to go in circles | Your hand pushing it outwards all the time
exercise: What plays the part of the string for the Moon? | The Earth's gravity | The Sun's light | The Moon's own spin | Nothing, it needs no string
exercise: You let go of the string. Which way does the ball fly? | Off in a straight line | On round in a curve | Straight back to your hand | Straight down at your feet
exercise: Why do astronauts float inside the space station? | They and the station are falling round the Earth together | There is no gravity at all in space | The station is above the reach of gravity | They are lighter than the air inside
exercise: How strong is gravity at the space station, compared with on the ground? | Almost as strong | Exactly zero | About one sixth as strong | Twice as strong
exercise: How much would you weigh on the Moon? | About one sixth of your Earth weight | Nothing at all | Exactly the same as on Earth, to the gram | About twice your Earth weight
exercise: What stays the same about you on the Moon? | Your mass, the amount of stuff in you | Your weight on the scales | How high you can jump | How hard the ground pulls you down to it
exercise: What do bathroom scales really measure? | How hard you press down on them | How much air is inside your body | How tall and how wide your body is | How fast your heart is beating today
exercise: Why doesn't the Earth fall into the Sun? | Its sideways speed keeps it going round | The Sun pushes the Earth away | The Earth is too far for the Sun's pull | Space is too thick to fall through
exercise: Who showed that the falling apple and the Moon obey the same pull? | Isaac Newton | Galileo | Albert Einstein | Neil Armstrong
exercise: What keeps a phone's satellite up there without an engine? | It falls round the Earth fast enough to keep missing it | It floats because it is very light | It sits on top of the air | Huge magnets on the ground hold it
exercise: What is a shooting star really? | A tiny bit of space rock glowing in the air | A real star falling right out of the night sky | A satellite switching its lights on | A spark from a far-off volcano
exercise: Why does the water stay in a bucket swung fast over your head? | The bucket's bottom keeps pushing it round | Gravity switches off at the top | The air holds the water in | The water sticks to the bottom of the bucket
exercise: Why don't riders fall out at the top of a roller coaster loop? | The car is fast enough that the track keeps pushing it round | Gravity does not work upside down | The loop is full of strong wind | The riders are too light to fall
test: Is there no gravity in space, like my cousin says? | There is; astronauts float because they fall together | Right, gravity stops where the air stops | Right, gravity only works on the ground | There is, but only near the Sun
test: In a film the hero lets go of a sling and the stone curves round the corner. What would really happen? | It flies off in a straight line | It keeps curving round in a circle | It drops straight down to the ground at once | It flies straight back to the hero
test: What would happen to the Moon if the Earth's gravity switched off? | It would fly away in a straight line | It would fall onto the Earth | It would stop dead in space | It would spin faster and faster round us
test: lol on the moon ur not lighter u just feel lighter. right? | No, you really weigh less, but your mass stays | Yes, your weight stays exactly the same | No, you weigh nothing at all there | Yes, only your mass gets smaller
test: You stand on scales in a lift that suddenly starts going down. What do the scales show for a moment? | Less than usual | More than usual | Exactly the same | Zero, all the time
test: A lift's cable snaps. Why would the people inside feel like floating for a moment? | They and the lift fall together | Gravity stops working inside a lift | Air rushes in and holds them up high | The lift goes up first, then down
test: A satellite slows down a lot. What happens to it? | It drops into the air and burns up | It floats still, staying where it is | It flies off towards the Moon instead | It falls gently down like a feather
test: Why would you hardly be able to lift your feet on Jupiter? | Jupiter pulls more than twice as hard as Earth | Jupiter's thick air holds your feet down like glue | Your mass doubles on Jupiter | Jupiter spins your feet down
test: crap, i swung the bucket too slow at the top. what happens? | The water pours on your head | Nothing, water never falls out | The bucket gets lighter | The water freezes in place
test: Why do roller coaster riders get strapped in, though the car is going fast? | In case the car is not fast enough at the top | Because gravity is stronger in loops | To stop them floating up into the sky at the top | Because the track pushes them out
test: What makes a shooting star glow? | The air in front of it gets squashed and very hot | It is burning like a real star | It reflects light from the Moon | It has a tiny battery inside it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition of astronauts aboard an orbiting space station? | They are in continual free fall about the Earth together with the station | They are beyond the reach of terrestrial gravitation | They are sustained by the pressure of the cabin air | They are repelled by the gravitation of the Sun
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the Earth does not descend into the Sun. | Its lateral velocity continually carries it past the Sun | The Sun exerts a repulsive force upon the Earth | The intervening space impedes all motion | The gravitation of the Sun does not extend so far
exam: It is given that a person is transported to the surface of the Moon. Determine which quantity remains unaltered. | The mass of the person | The weight of the person | The reading of a spring balance | The height attainable in a jump
exam: Ascertain the trajectory of a ball swung upon a cord in a circle at the instant at which the cord is released. | A straight line | A continuation of the circle | A vertical descent to the ground | A return towards the hand
